Twine The Video Game Technology for all 
Developers Zoe Quinn, Patrick Lindsey and Isaac Schankler created the text based game “Depression Quest” on the platform Twine. “Depression Quest” invites players via hyperlinks to step into the shoes of someone suffering from clinical depression. It’s an empathy exercise that doesn’t have a happy ending or resolution. The developers received a lot of backlash once the game was released, people believed it to be too inclusive and politicized for gamer culture. It is a misconception that gamers are only middle class men. “Nearly 50 percent of people now playing games are female, according to the Entertainment Software Association. Even more surprising, there are more adult women playing than there are boys under 18.” (Pg. 3/6) 
“Twine represents something even more radical: the transformation of video games into something that is not only consumed by the masses but also created by them.”(pg. 2/6) We live in a world being overtaken by the digital realm. I see it in my students who are in their early 20s, some are shy to interact with each other, but many of them play video games. Video games that can create an experience of empathy or teach the players about someone else’s experience are a powerful tool to prevent people from complete isolation. I think it’s amazing that platforms like Twine are accessible to creators of different backgrounds and perspectives. 
